ing crates from the Portland wharf into a powered underground refrigeration vault—and finds her father's original 1998 binder open to a passenger-and-specimen log from a ship that supposedly sank 26 years ago.2. MASTER CHRONOLOGICAL TIMELINEERA 1: The Barrow Island Origin & Vance Maritime (1984 – ~2018)1984: Rhode Island decommissions Quarantine Station No. 4 (B.I.Q.S.-04), a nineteenth-century brick contagious-disease hospital and morgue on Barrow Island in lower Narragansett Bay. The island is sold off to a private Providence maritime shell trust.1998:A vessel supposedly sinks off Block Island.Vance Maritime & Cargo (V.M.C.), a three-man marine surveying firm on Custom House Wharf in Portland, Maine, run by Clara and Maya's father, audits the initial offload at Barrow Island (V.M.C. — AUDIT 1998).Six refrigerated steel containment crates are offloaded into the decommissioned basement morgue of Ward C on Barrow Island (B.I.Q.S.-04 — WARD C), along with a bonded passenger-and-specimen transfer log from the "sunken" Block Island vessel.The 1998 offload sheet is countersigned by Victor Haldane (later Chief Marine Underwriter at Harbor Mutual Insurance).The calfskin ledger used to track the off-book escrow disbursements is stamped on its back cover with the steel notary seal: QUARANTINE STATION NO. 4 — BARROW ISLAND, R.I. and V.M.C. — AUDIT 1998.Clara's Sophomore Year of College (~2014–2016):Clara and Maya's father dies suddenly of a coronary. Vance Maritime & Cargo folds.Clara keeps her father's estate boxes, brass parallel ruler, and cracked leather logbook in the spare room of her Portland apartment, unaware that Maya later found the missing 1998 carbon sheet and the WARD C brass key among his papers.ERA 2: The VaneCor Trial Deaths & Maya's Disappearance (August – October 2020)August – October 2020:21-year-old Maya Vance works an internship in a Burlington, Vermont biostatistics office auditing clinical trials for Vane Biotech, owned by Arthur Vane.Sloane Mercer-Hall (Managing Partner at Mercer-Hall Fiduciary on State Street in Boston) underwrites a $38,000,000 institutional bond keeping Vane Biotech afloat after the FDA delays approval of its cardiac drug, VaneCor.Three children in Burlington and St. Johnsbury enrolled in the VaneCor pediatric trial (batches VC-09-412 and VC-09-418) suffer fatal QT-interval arrhythmia spikes within 72 hours of an escalated dose.October 14, 2020:To prevent the pediatric deaths from hitting the federal registry and collapsing the $38M bond pool, Arthur Vane and Sloane Mercer-Hall co-sign off-book payouts (starting with Case #VT-20-114 for $145,000) out of State Street Escrow Account #884-19B.Arthur's winter caretaker and courier, Hollis Greer (identifiable by the missing distal tip of his right ring finger), delivers cash envelopes to rural county coroners to reclassify the children's deaths as "accidental hypothermia" or "winter creek drowning."Late October 2020:Working late on a Tuesday night, Maya discovers the raw pediatric EKG variance strips and a copied Mercer-Hall escrow slip. She also connects Vane Biotech's ledger back to her father's 1998 Barrow Island audit (B.I.Q.S.-04) and Victor Haldane in Portland.Maya hides the B.I.Q.S.-04 — WARD C key and her father's 1998 survey carbon sheet inside a rusted steel lockbox in a rented storage unit on Shelburne Road in Burlington.Three days after pulling the EKG logs, while Maya is at a Pine Street coin laundromat, Hollis Greer douses her Burlington apartment in lamp kerosene and burns it down. Maya spots Hollis's four-fingered right hand on the steering wheel of his pickup outside the fire.Within 12 hours, Vane Biotech files a felony complaint framing Maya for data theft. Realizing that calling Clara would lead Hollis Greer and Victor Haldane straight to Clara's door in Portland, Maya cuts her hair in a bus-station sink, boards a midnight Greyhound with $40, and spends the next six years working off-grid archival jobs across New England under a